>      The original concept for magic cancellation--somewhat unfortunate
> terminology which I think tends to lead to confusion; I prefer magic
> negation but I don't think that'll ever catch on--is that your body
> is so thoroughly covered that the attack doesn't reach your skin.  But
> then it has been tweaked for play balance with the result that it's
> not very consistent.  And the cornuthaum threw things completely out
> of whack.  Perhaps characters in funny hats cause monsters to
> subconsciously target those hats; why it doesn't work for the dunce
> cap is left as an exercise for the reader.

Huh. I had never thought of magic cancellation that way (and to be
fair, in the current state of the game, there seems little to suggest
it). As it stands, it seems like a bizarre secondary form of magic
resistance. Really, I think "Special Attack Prevention Level" would be
the clearest moniker, but it doesn't have the same ring to it.

>
>      Making magic cancellation become an innate attribute for certain
> naked characters would be a complete contradiction to the original
> concept.  Having some character get innate half spell damage and/or
> half physical damage would be more reasonable, but I can't figure out
> why lack of armor should be a factor or even why it would apply to
> this particular role over others.

Well, the idea I was working from with the barbarian was a sort of
specialised form of luck. In fantasy fiction and other media, the
barbarian hero is almost always insufficiently protected for the peril
they are facing and yet all the should-be-fatal dangers seem to just
miss them for no reason other than their inherent badassness. The
vampire's teeth fail to sink in, not because of adequate neck
protection, but rather just because he's CONAN, god dammit. :)

>      An idea I've been toying with for nethack is to make the only
> way to achieve MC3 be to wear a cloak of protection or a ring of
> protection.  (This may never happen; if it ever does, I'd probably
> introduce an amulet of protection to provide another alternative.)
> Divine protection, spell of protection, or successfully consuming
> rings of protection would only enhance your armor class, not provide
> magic negation.  Players who are obsessed with MC3 would have to
> change their ascension kits; those who have decided that MC2 is good
> enough wouldn't be affected.  (Enlightenment feedback has already
> been enhanced to include magic negation, with "you are X" where X is
> "warded" or "guarded" or something ["protected"?].  I don't remember
> the exact terminology; it doesn't mention internal numeric values.)

Making MC3 harder to get (and/or less failsafe, a la sporkhack) can
only be a good thing. If there was an attempt to get back to the
original concept of being thoroughly covered, one idea that would make
sense would be for any cloak to give one point of MC and having a full
suite of [boots, gloves, helmet, shirt/armor] give a second, stacking
to MC2. MC3 would then be limited to the magical forms of protection
you listed.


